'use strict';
const AIRWAY_DECK_SLIDES=[["Airway Management, Respiration, and Artificial Ventilation","Chapters 9–10. Use this companion with the original PowerPoint below."],["Upper and lower airway","Air enters through the nose or mouth, then passes through the pharynx and larynx to the trachea, bronchi, bronchioles and alveoli. Correction: the pharynx runs nasopharynx → oropharynx → laryngopharynx from superior to inferior. Slide 2 reverses the first two regions. Air from the mouth enters at the oropharynx."],["Anatomy of the respiratory system","This slide links to an anatomy video. Review the route from nose/mouth to alveoli, and distinguish conducting passages from the alveolar gas-exchange surface. See the original slide for its linked resource."],["Pediatric airway","Children have smaller, narrower and less rigid airways, a proportionately larger tongue, a softer trachea and chest wall, and greater dependence on the diaphragm. Infants preferentially breathe through the nose. Swelling or secretions can markedly narrow a small airway."],["Breathing mechanics","This slide uses visual resources to introduce inhalation and exhalation. Compare the expanding chest and falling intrathoracic pressure of spontaneous inhalation with the relaxing chest of quiet exhalation."],["Ventilation and volumes","Spontaneous inhalation is active: diaphragm contraction expands the chest and lowers pressure, drawing air in. Quiet exhalation is usually passive. Tidal volume is air per breath. Minute ventilation = rate × tidal volume. Alveolar ventilation = rate × (tidal volume − dead-space volume). Fast, shallow breaths can move little fresh air to alveoli."],["Breathing physiology","At the alveolar-capillary interface, O₂ moves into blood and CO₂ moves into alveoli along partial-pressure gradients. Adequate ventilation and perfusion must work together. The deck uses “cellular respiration” for tissue gas exchange; distinguish tissue gas exchange from the metabolic processes inside cells."],["Gas exchange and the cardiopulmonary system","The slide's visual resources connect alveoli, pulmonary capillaries, the heart and body tissues. Follow oxygen toward tissues and carbon dioxide back toward the lungs."],["Gas exchange","Gases cross the respiratory membrane by simple diffusion. Partial-pressure gradients, membrane surface area and ventilation/perfusion matching affect exchange."],["When things go wrong","Hypoxia means inadequate tissue oxygen. Hypercapnia means elevated CO₂. Increased rate and effort may compensate initially. Worsening mental status, exhaustion and inadequate breathing suggest failure. Normal SpO₂ alone cannot exclude ventilatory failure, especially with supplemental oxygen. Respiratory distress does not guarantee normal color, mental status or SpO₂."],["Airway sounds","Stridor suggests upper-airway narrowing. Hoarseness suggests a laryngeal/voice change. Snoring can reflect soft-tissue obstruction with reduced tone. Gurgling suggests fluid such as blood, vomit or secretions. These sounds are clues, not stand-alone diagnoses."],["Ongoing airway assessment","Airway patency can change as consciousness, muscle tone, swelling or secretions change. Reassess airway, breathing and circulation after interventions and whenever the patient changes."],["Airway management","Position and open the airway, clear secretions, choose adjuncts and support breathing according to assessment and protocol. Head tilt–chin lift is used when cervical injury is not suspected; trained rescuers use jaw thrust initially with suspected trauma. If ineffective, prioritize opening the airway. OPA use requires absence of a gag reflex. Follow device-specific sizing and contraindications. Remove only visible foreign material; never perform a blind finger sweep."],["Nonrebreather mask","A nonrebreather provides supplemental oxygen to a patient who is breathing. It does not replace ventilation when breathing is inadequate. The slide's 25 L/min example is not a universal setting. Use your device instructions and local protocol; maintain reservoir inflation. Improvised mask connections are not generalized as instructions here."],["Small-volume nebulizer","Identify the medication chamber, mouthpiece or approved mask, tubing and oxygen supply. The deck describes 6–8 L/min for common jet nebulizers. Actual flow and compatible parts depend on the manufacturer and setup."],["Nebulizer with CPAP or BVM","The instructor describes inline aerosol delivery with positive-pressure equipment. Use only approved device configurations and local protocols. Oxygen-source requirements vary by equipment. Correction: DuoNeb contains both ipratropium and albuterol; it is not albuterol alone. Positive pressure does not guarantee improved medication deposition."],["CPAP","CPAP supplies continuous positive airway pressure to selected patients who can breathe spontaneously and meet protocol criteria. Explain the mask and pressure, coach the patient and reassess tolerance and condition. It is not a substitute for assisted ventilation in a patient unable to breathe adequately or protect the airway."],["BVM","Bag-mask ventilation supports inadequate or absent breathing. Airway positioning, a good mask seal, visible chest rise and repeated reassessment matter. The slide also mentions nebulizer and supraglottic connections, which require approved equipment and authorized training."],["Waveform capnography","The deck lists a usual adult ETCO₂ reference range of 35–45 mmHg. Interpret the waveform, trend and clinical context together. ETCO₂ reflects ventilation, perfusion and metabolism, so a number alone does not establish a diagnosis."],["Respiratory severity","The original slide contains a 2022 severity-assessment table. Look for reduced speech, accessory-muscle use, altered mental status, exhaustion and poor air movement. The table is a course reference, not a universal current treatment protocol. Position according to the patient's needs and reassess."],["Airway protection vs patency","Patency means the passage is open. Protection means the patient can keep material such as vomit and secretions out of the lower airway. An open airway does not guarantee protective reflexes. Assess both, repeatedly."],["Review resources","The final slide points to a Quizlet resource. Use the original deck for its embedded links, then test recall with the question bank and exercises on this page."]];
window.STUDY_ITEMS=window.STUDY_ITEMS||[];
window.STUDY_ITEMS.push(...[{"id":"airway-deck-q1","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Secondary Assessment","title":"Which sequence correctly orders the pharynx from superior to inferior?","options":["Nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx","Oropharynx, nasopharynx, laryngopharynx","Laryngopharynx, nasopharynx, oropharynx","Trachea, oropharynx, nasopharynx"],"correct":0,"body":"Slide 2 reverses the first two regions. The nasopharynx is behind the nose; the oropharynx is behind the mouth.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 2 · clarified wording","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q2","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Secondary Assessment","title":"Which structures surround the alveoli and receive oxygen?","options":["Pulmonary capillaries","Vocal cords","Nasal turbinates","Esophagus"],"correct":0,"body":"Gas crosses the thin alveolar-capillary interface.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 2 · clarified wording","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q3","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Secondary Assessment","title":"Which airway route follows the trachea toward gas exchange?","options":["Bronchi, bronchioles, alveoli","Alveoli, larynx, nose","Esophagus, stomach, alveoli","Pharynx, mouth, capillaries"],"correct":0,"body":"Conducting passages branch into smaller airways and terminate at gas-exchange regions.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 2 · clarified wording","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q4","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Secondary Assessment","title":"Why can modest swelling be especially serious in a child's airway?","options":["The airway starts with a smaller internal diameter","Children have no diaphragm","The airway is wider than in adults","Swelling improves airflow"],"correct":0,"body":"A smaller airway can become markedly narrowed or obstructed.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 4","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q5","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Secondary Assessment","title":"Which feature is proportionately larger in a pediatric airway?","options":["Tongue","Adult-sized trachea","Lung capacity","Chest-wall rigidity"],"correct":0,"body":"The tongue is proportionately larger; the airway and chest wall are less rigid.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 4","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q6","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"During spontaneous inhalation, what draws air inward?","options":["Chest expansion lowers intrathoracic pressure","The diaphragm relaxes and raises chest pressure","The lungs stop expanding","Pulmonary capillaries squeeze air in"],"correct":0,"body":"Diaphragm contraction expands the chest and creates a pressure gradient.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 6","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q7","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Quiet exhalation is usually:","options":["Passive as respiratory muscles relax","An active diaphragm contraction","A process with no pressure change","The same as tissue perfusion"],"correct":0,"body":"This describes quiet breathing; forced exhalation can involve active muscles.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 6","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q8","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"What is tidal volume?","options":["Air moved in one breath","Air moved in one minute","Only the air remaining after exhalation","Number of breaths per minute"],"correct":0,"body":"Tidal volume is measured per breath.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 6","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q9","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"At 12 breaths/min and 500 mL/breath, minute ventilation is:","options":["6 L/min","0.6 L/min","12 L/min","500 L/min"],"correct":0,"body":"12 × 500 = 6000 mL/min = 6 L/min.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 6","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q10","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Assume tidal volume 500 mL, dead space 150 mL and rate 12/min. Alveolar ventilation is:","options":["4.2 L/min","6 L/min","1.8 L/min","42 L/min"],"correct":0,"body":"12 × (500 − 150) = 4200 mL/min = 4.2 L/min. Dead space is an assumed value for this exercise.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 6","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q11","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Two patterns have equal minute ventilation. Why can shallow, rapid breathing provide less alveolar ventilation?","options":["Dead space consumes a larger fraction of each smaller breath","Dead space disappears at faster rates","Rate alone determines effective ventilation","Every inhaled milliliter reaches the alveoli"],"correct":0,"body":"Alveolar ventilation depends on volume beyond dead space as well as rate.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 6","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q12","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Across the alveolar-capillary membrane, oxygen normally moves:","options":["From alveoli into pulmonary blood","From blood into the stomach","From tissues directly into inhaled air","Only when actively pumped across the membrane"],"correct":0,"body":"Oxygen diffuses down its partial-pressure gradient.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 7","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q13","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Carbon dioxide normally moves at the lungs:","options":["From pulmonary blood into alveoli","From alveoli into venous blood only","From the mouth into tissues","Without any pressure gradient"],"correct":0,"body":"CO₂ diffuses toward the alveolar gas to be exhaled.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 7","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q14","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Which process moves respiratory gases across the respiratory membrane?","options":["Simple diffusion","Osmosis of oxygen","Active pumping by alveoli","Swallowing"],"correct":0,"body":"The slide references a resource named Osmosis, but the gas-transfer process is diffusion.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 9","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q15","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Ventilation/perfusion matching requires:","options":["Air reaching alveoli and blood reaching their capillaries","Airway movement without circulation","Blood flow without ventilation","Only a normal respiratory rate"],"correct":0,"body":"Both ventilation and perfusion contribute to gas exchange.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 7","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q16","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Hypercapnia means:","options":["Elevated carbon dioxide","Low blood sugar","Low temperature","Excess oxygen alone"],"correct":0,"body":"Hypercapnia refers to increased CO₂.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 10","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q17","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"A patient on oxygen has SpO₂ 98% but becomes drowsy with slow, shallow breathing. What is the best interpretation?","options":["Normal SpO₂ does not rule out inadequate ventilation","Ventilation must be adequate because SpO₂ is normal","The airway needs no reassessment","Supplemental oxygen always removes CO₂"],"correct":0,"body":"Assess airway and adequacy of ventilation; oximetry alone does not assess CO₂ clearance.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 10","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q18","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Which change is especially concerning for progression toward respiratory failure?","options":["Exhaustion, reduced responsiveness and inadequate breathing","Comfortable speech with unchanged effort","A single normal pulse-ox reading","Improved chest rise after support"],"correct":0,"body":"Failure occurs when breathing can no longer meet the patient's needs.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 10","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q19","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"A high-pitched sound suggesting upper-airway narrowing is:","options":["Stridor","Gurgling","Snoring","Normal quiet airflow"],"correct":0,"body":"Stridor is an important upper-airway warning sign.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 11","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q20","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Bubbling sounds with secretions suggest:","options":["Gurgling","Hoarseness","Normal gas exchange","Isolated bronchospasm without fluid"],"correct":0,"body":"Assess for blood, vomit or secretions and clear the airway as indicated.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 11","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q21","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Snoring in a patient with reduced responsiveness may indicate:","options":["Soft-tissue airway obstruction from reduced tone","A guaranteed protected airway","Normal ventilation in every case","An isolated capillary problem"],"correct":0,"body":"Reposition/open and reassess the airway as indicated.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 11","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q22","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"After opening an airway, what comes next?","options":["Continue reassessing patency and breathing","Assume it will stay open","Wait until hospital arrival to reassess","Finish history before responding to deterioration"],"correct":0,"body":"Airway management is an ongoing process.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 12","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q23","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"An OPA is generally appropriate only when:","options":["The patient lacks a gag reflex","A strong gag reflex is present","The patient is talking normally","It is used as a substitute for ventilation"],"correct":0,"body":"An intact gag reflex is a contraindication. Follow training and device guidance.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 13 · clarified wording","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q24","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"With suspected head/neck trauma, trained rescuers initially use:","options":["Jaw thrust without head extension","Routine head rotation","A blind finger sweep","A forceful head tilt in every case"],"correct":0,"body":"If jaw thrust and adjuncts do not open the airway, current AHA guidance prioritizes opening it, including head tilt–chin lift when needed.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 13 · clarified wording","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q25","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"When should you perform a blind finger sweep?","options":["Never; remove foreign material only when visible","Whenever the patient snores","Before every ventilation","Whenever an OPA is unavailable"],"correct":0,"body":"Blind sweeps can worsen obstruction. Use the appropriate choking protocol.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 13 · clarified wording","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q26","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"What is the key limitation of a nonrebreather mask?","options":["It supplies oxygen but does not provide assisted ventilation","It always removes retained CO₂","It guarantees adequate tidal volume","It replaces airway positioning"],"correct":0,"body":"A patient with inadequate breathing may need ventilatory support, rather than simply a higher oxygen flow.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 14","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q27","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"The deck gives 6–8 L/min for a common jet nebulizer. How should this be applied?","options":["Verify the actual device's instructions and protocol","Use the same flow with every device","Always exceed the labeled flow","Treat the number as a medication dose"],"correct":0,"body":"Nebulizer performance and flow requirements are equipment-specific.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 15","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q28","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"DuoNeb contains which medications?","options":["Ipratropium and albuterol","Albuterol alone","Epinephrine and glucose","Oxygen and magnesium"],"correct":0,"body":"The speaker note calls DuoNeb nebulized albuterol, but it contains two active bronchodilators.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 16 · clarified wording","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q29","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Which patient characteristic is important when considering CPAP?","options":["Ability to breathe spontaneously and meet protocol criteria","Complete apnea","Inability to protect the airway","No need for reassessment"],"correct":0,"body":"Patient selection, contraindications, tolerance and reassessment matter.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 17","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q30","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"During bag-mask ventilation, which finding helps assess delivery of a breath?","options":["Visible chest rise","Only squeezing the entire bag forcefully","Only a high oxygen-flow setting","Only the sound of the bag refilling"],"correct":0,"body":"Assess seal, airway position, chest rise and patient response. Avoid excessive ventilation.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 18","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q31","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Secondary Assessment","title":"The deck's usual adult ETCO₂ reference range is:","options":["35–45 mmHg","35–45 percent","5–10 mg/dL","95–100 mmHg"],"correct":0,"body":"ETCO₂ is expressed as pressure, commonly mmHg. Interpret in context.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 19","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q32","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Secondary Assessment","title":"Which statement about capnography is best?","options":["Interpret waveform, trend and patient findings together","One number proves a diagnosis","ETCO₂ depends only on respiratory rate","Capnography eliminates reassessment"],"correct":0,"body":"Ventilation, perfusion and metabolism all affect the reading.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 19","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q33","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"A wheezing patient becomes exhausted, speaks only single words and has poor air movement. What is the priority?","options":["Reassess ABCs and support airway/breathing as indicated","Assume less wheezing means improvement","Delay care for a complete history","Judge severity only from a previous diagnosis"],"correct":0,"body":"Poor air movement and exhaustion can indicate deterioration even if wheezing becomes quieter.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 20","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-q34","type":"question","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"What is the difference between airway patency and protection?","options":["Patency is an open passage; protection helps prevent aspiration","They always mean the same thing","An OPA always guarantees protection","A normal SpO₂ guarantees protective reflexes"],"correct":0,"body":"An open passage does not establish that the patient can protect it from secretions or vomit.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 21","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c1","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Secondary Assessment","title":"Which sequence correctly orders the pharynx from superior to inferior?","options":["Nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx","Oropharynx, nasopharynx, laryngopharynx","Laryngopharynx, nasopharynx, oropharynx","Trachea, oropharynx, nasopharynx"],"correct":0,"body":"Nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx. Slide 2 reverses the first two regions. The nasopharynx is behind the nose; the oropharynx is behind the mouth.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 2 · clarified wording","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c2","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Secondary Assessment","title":"Which structures surround the alveoli and receive oxygen?","options":["Pulmonary capillaries","Vocal cords","Nasal turbinates","Esophagus"],"correct":0,"body":"Pulmonary capillaries. Gas crosses the thin alveolar-capillary interface.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 2 · clarified wording","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c3","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Secondary Assessment","title":"Which airway route follows the trachea toward gas exchange?","options":["Bronchi, bronchioles, alveoli","Alveoli, larynx, nose","Esophagus, stomach, alveoli","Pharynx, mouth, capillaries"],"correct":0,"body":"Bronchi, bronchioles, alveoli. Conducting passages branch into smaller airways and terminate at gas-exchange regions.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 2 · clarified wording","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c4","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Secondary Assessment","title":"Why can modest swelling be especially serious in a child's airway?","options":["The airway starts with a smaller internal diameter","Children have no diaphragm","The airway is wider than in adults","Swelling improves airflow"],"correct":0,"body":"The airway starts with a smaller internal diameter. A smaller airway can become markedly narrowed or obstructed.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 4","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c5","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Secondary Assessment","title":"Which feature is proportionately larger in a pediatric airway?","options":["Tongue","Adult-sized trachea","Lung capacity","Chest-wall rigidity"],"correct":0,"body":"Tongue. The tongue is proportionately larger; the airway and chest wall are less rigid.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 4","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c6","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"During spontaneous inhalation, what draws air inward?","options":["Chest expansion lowers intrathoracic pressure","The diaphragm relaxes and raises chest pressure","The lungs stop expanding","Pulmonary capillaries squeeze air in"],"correct":0,"body":"Chest expansion lowers intrathoracic pressure. Diaphragm contraction expands the chest and creates a pressure gradient.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 6","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c7","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Quiet exhalation is usually:","options":["Passive as respiratory muscles relax","An active diaphragm contraction","A process with no pressure change","The same as tissue perfusion"],"correct":0,"body":"Passive as respiratory muscles relax. This describes quiet breathing; forced exhalation can involve active muscles.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 6","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c8","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"What is tidal volume?","options":["Air moved in one breath","Air moved in one minute","Only the air remaining after exhalation","Number of breaths per minute"],"correct":0,"body":"Air moved in one breath. Tidal volume is measured per breath.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 6","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c9","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"At 12 breaths/min and 500 mL/breath, minute ventilation is:","options":["6 L/min","0.6 L/min","12 L/min","500 L/min"],"correct":0,"body":"6 L/min. 12 × 500 = 6000 mL/min = 6 L/min.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 6","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c10","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Assume tidal volume 500 mL, dead space 150 mL and rate 12/min. Alveolar ventilation is:","options":["4.2 L/min","6 L/min","1.8 L/min","42 L/min"],"correct":0,"body":"4.2 L/min. 12 × (500 − 150) = 4200 mL/min = 4.2 L/min. Dead space is an assumed value for this exercise.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 6","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c11","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Two patterns have equal minute ventilation. Why can shallow, rapid breathing provide less alveolar ventilation?","options":["Dead space consumes a larger fraction of each smaller breath","Dead space disappears at faster rates","Rate alone determines effective ventilation","Every inhaled milliliter reaches the alveoli"],"correct":0,"body":"Dead space consumes a larger fraction of each smaller breath. Alveolar ventilation depends on volume beyond dead space as well as rate.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 6","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c12","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Across the alveolar-capillary membrane, oxygen normally moves:","options":["From alveoli into pulmonary blood","From blood into the stomach","From tissues directly into inhaled air","Only when actively pumped across the membrane"],"correct":0,"body":"From alveoli into pulmonary blood. Oxygen diffuses down its partial-pressure gradient.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 7","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c13","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Carbon dioxide normally moves at the lungs:","options":["From pulmonary blood into alveoli","From alveoli into venous blood only","From the mouth into tissues","Without any pressure gradient"],"correct":0,"body":"From pulmonary blood into alveoli. CO₂ diffuses toward the alveolar gas to be exhaled.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 7","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c14","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Which process moves respiratory gases across the respiratory membrane?","options":["Simple diffusion","Osmosis of oxygen","Active pumping by alveoli","Swallowing"],"correct":0,"body":"Simple diffusion. The slide references a resource named Osmosis, but the gas-transfer process is diffusion.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 9","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c15","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Ventilation/perfusion matching requires:","options":["Air reaching alveoli and blood reaching their capillaries","Airway movement without circulation","Blood flow without ventilation","Only a normal respiratory rate"],"correct":0,"body":"Air reaching alveoli and blood reaching their capillaries. Both ventilation and perfusion contribute to gas exchange.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 7","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c16","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Hypercapnia means:","options":["Elevated carbon dioxide","Low blood sugar","Low temperature","Excess oxygen alone"],"correct":0,"body":"Elevated carbon dioxide. Hypercapnia refers to increased CO₂.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 10","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c17","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"A patient on oxygen has SpO₂ 98% but becomes drowsy with slow, shallow breathing. What is the best interpretation?","options":["Normal SpO₂ does not rule out inadequate ventilation","Ventilation must be adequate because SpO₂ is normal","The airway needs no reassessment","Supplemental oxygen always removes CO₂"],"correct":0,"body":"Normal SpO₂ does not rule out inadequate ventilation. Assess airway and adequacy of ventilation; oximetry alone does not assess CO₂ clearance.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 10","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c18","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Which change is especially concerning for progression toward respiratory failure?","options":["Exhaustion, reduced responsiveness and inadequate breathing","Comfortable speech with unchanged effort","A single normal pulse-ox reading","Improved chest rise after support"],"correct":0,"body":"Exhaustion, reduced responsiveness and inadequate breathing. Failure occurs when breathing can no longer meet the patient's needs.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 10","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c19","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"A high-pitched sound suggesting upper-airway narrowing is:","options":["Stridor","Gurgling","Snoring","Normal quiet airflow"],"correct":0,"body":"Stridor. Stridor is an important upper-airway warning sign.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 11","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c20","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Bubbling sounds with secretions suggest:","options":["Gurgling","Hoarseness","Normal gas exchange","Isolated bronchospasm without fluid"],"correct":0,"body":"Gurgling. Assess for blood, vomit or secretions and clear the airway as indicated.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 11","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c21","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Snoring in a patient with reduced responsiveness may indicate:","options":["Soft-tissue airway obstruction from reduced tone","A guaranteed protected airway","Normal ventilation in every case","An isolated capillary problem"],"correct":0,"body":"Soft-tissue airway obstruction from reduced tone. Reposition/open and reassess the airway as indicated.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 11","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c22","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"After opening an airway, what comes next?","options":["Continue reassessing patency and breathing","Assume it will stay open","Wait until hospital arrival to reassess","Finish history before responding to deterioration"],"correct":0,"body":"Continue reassessing patency and breathing. Airway management is an ongoing process.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 12","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c23","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"An OPA is generally appropriate only when:","options":["The patient lacks a gag reflex","A strong gag reflex is present","The patient is talking normally","It is used as a substitute for ventilation"],"correct":0,"body":"The patient lacks a gag reflex. An intact gag reflex is a contraindication. Follow training and device guidance.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 13 · clarified wording","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c24","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"With suspected head/neck trauma, trained rescuers initially use:","options":["Jaw thrust without head extension","Routine head rotation","A blind finger sweep","A forceful head tilt in every case"],"correct":0,"body":"Jaw thrust without head extension. If jaw thrust and adjuncts do not open the airway, current AHA guidance prioritizes opening it, including head tilt–chin lift when needed.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 13 · clarified wording","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c25","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"When should you perform a blind finger sweep?","options":["Never; remove foreign material only when visible","Whenever the patient snores","Before every ventilation","Whenever an OPA is unavailable"],"correct":0,"body":"Never; remove foreign material only when visible. Blind sweeps can worsen obstruction. Use the appropriate choking protocol.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 13 · clarified wording","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c26","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"What is the key limitation of a nonrebreather mask?","options":["It supplies oxygen but does not provide assisted ventilation","It always removes retained CO₂","It guarantees adequate tidal volume","It replaces airway positioning"],"correct":0,"body":"It supplies oxygen but does not provide assisted ventilation. A patient with inadequate breathing may need ventilatory support, rather than simply a higher oxygen flow.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 14","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c27","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"The deck gives 6–8 L/min for a common jet nebulizer. How should this be applied?","options":["Verify the actual device's instructions and protocol","Use the same flow with every device","Always exceed the labeled flow","Treat the number as a medication dose"],"correct":0,"body":"Verify the actual device's instructions and protocol. Nebulizer performance and flow requirements are equipment-specific.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 15","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c28","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"DuoNeb contains which medications?","options":["Ipratropium and albuterol","Albuterol alone","Epinephrine and glucose","Oxygen and magnesium"],"correct":0,"body":"Ipratropium and albuterol. The speaker note calls DuoNeb nebulized albuterol, but it contains two active bronchodilators.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 16 · clarified wording","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c29","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"Which patient characteristic is important when considering CPAP?","options":["Ability to breathe spontaneously and meet protocol criteria","Complete apnea","Inability to protect the airway","No need for reassessment"],"correct":0,"body":"Ability to breathe spontaneously and meet protocol criteria. Patient selection, contraindications, tolerance and reassessment matter.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 17","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c30","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"During bag-mask ventilation, which finding helps assess delivery of a breath?","options":["Visible chest rise","Only squeezing the entire bag forcefully","Only a high oxygen-flow setting","Only the sound of the bag refilling"],"correct":0,"body":"Visible chest rise. Assess seal, airway position, chest rise and patient response. Avoid excessive ventilation.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 18","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c31","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Secondary Assessment","title":"The deck's usual adult ETCO₂ reference range is:","options":["35–45 mmHg","35–45 percent","5–10 mg/dL","95–100 mmHg"],"correct":0,"body":"35–45 mmHg. ETCO₂ is expressed as pressure, commonly mmHg. Interpret in context.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 19","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c32","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Secondary Assessment","title":"Which statement about capnography is best?","options":["Interpret waveform, trend and patient findings together","One number proves a diagnosis","ETCO₂ depends only on respiratory rate","Capnography eliminates reassessment"],"correct":0,"body":"Interpret waveform, trend and patient findings together. Ventilation, perfusion and metabolism all affect the reading.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 19","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c33","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"A wheezing patient becomes exhausted, speaks only single words and has poor air movement. What is the priority?","options":["Reassess ABCs and support airway/breathing as indicated","Assume less wheezing means improvement","Delay care for a complete history","Judge severity only from a previous diagnosis"],"correct":0,"body":"Reassess ABCs and support airway/breathing as indicated. Poor air movement and exhaustion can indicate deterioration even if wheezing becomes quieter.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 20","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"},{"id":"airway-deck-c34","type":"card","set":"airway-deck","topic":"Airway slide deck","domain":"Primary Assessment","title":"What is the difference between airway patency and protection?","options":["Patency is an open passage; protection helps prevent aspiration","They always mean the same thing","An OPA always guarantees protection","A normal SpO₂ guarantees protective reflexes"],"correct":0,"body":"Patency is an open passage; protection helps prevent aspiration. An open passage does not establish that the patient can protect it from secretions or vomit.","source":"Airway Management course deck · slide 21","sourceUrl":"https://emt-study-room.vercel.app/#airway-slides"}]);
let airwaySlideIndex=0, airwayPathAnswers={}, airwayPathChecked=false;
const AIRWAY_PATH=['Trachea','Mainstem bronchi','Bronchioles','Alveoli'];
function airwayDeckStudy(){
app.innerHTML=heading('Airway, Respiration & Ventilation','Your 22-slide course deck, with active recall and applied practice.')+
'<section class="course-feature"><span class="tag">CHAPTERS 9–10 · 22 SLIDES</span><h2>Study the original, then test your recall</h2><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/resources/airway-management-respiration-ventilation.pptx" download>Download original PowerPoint ↓</a><button class="primary" onclick="navigate(\'airway-deck-quiz\')">Practice 34 questions →</button><button onclick="navigate(\'airway-deck-exam\')">Timed practice</button><button onclick="navigate(\'airway-deck-cards\')">34 flashcards</button></div><p class="storage-note">The original PowerPoint preserves the instructor’s slides and embedded links. The companion below condenses each slide and identifies corrections. Open the download in PowerPoint, Keynote or another compatible viewer.</p></section>'+
'<section class="contentcard"><label>Jump to slide <select onchange="airwaySlideIndex=Number(this.value);airwayDeckStudy()">'+AIRWAY_DECK_SLIDES.map((s,i)=>'<option value="'+i+'" '+(i===airwaySlideIndex?'selected':'')+'>'+(i+1)+'. '+esc(s[0])+'</option>').join('')+'</select></label><span class="tag">SLIDE '+(airwaySlideIndex+1)+' / 22</span><h2>'+esc(AIRWAY_DECK_SLIDES[airwaySlideIndex][0])+'</h2><figure style="margin:1rem 0"><a href="/diagrams/airway-slide-'+(airwaySlideIndex+1)+'.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/diagrams/airway-slide-'+(airwaySlideIndex+1)+'.jpg" width="1200" height="750" alt="Open full-size preview of slide '+(airwaySlideIndex+1)+': '+esc(AIRWAY_DECK_SLIDES[airwaySlideIndex][0])+'" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px"></a><figcaption class="storage-note">Reformatted preview of the PowerPoint slide. Select the image to enlarge it.</figcaption></figure><p class="note">'+esc(AIRWAY_DECK_SLIDES[airwaySlideIndex][1])+'</p><div class="actions"><button onclick="airwayDeckFlip(-1)" '+(airwaySlideIndex===0?'disabled':'')+'>← Previous</button><button onclick="airwayDeckFlip(1)" '+(airwaySlideIndex===21?'disabled':'')+'>Next slide →</button></div></section>'+
'<section class="contentcard"><span class="tag">SLIDES 2, 7–9 · AIRFLOW & GAS EXCHANGE</span><h2>Complete the pathway to the alveoli</h2><p>Nose / mouth → pharynx → larynx → fill the four structures below.</p><div class="airway-path" style="display:grid;gap:12px">'+AIRWAY_PATH.map((name,i)=>'<label>Step '+(i+1)+' <select id="airway-path-'+i+'" onchange="airwayPathAnswers['+i+']=this.value;airwayPathChecked=false"><option value="">Choose structure…</option>'+[...AIRWAY_PATH].sort().map(n=>'<option '+(airwayPathAnswers[i]===n?'selected':'')+'>'+esc(n)+'</option>').join('')+'</select></label>').join('')+'</div><div class="actions"><button class="primary" onclick="airwayCheckPath()">Check pathway</button><button onclick="airwayPathAnswers={};airwayPathChecked=false;airwayDeckStudy()">Reset</button></div><p id="airway-path-status" role="status">'+(airwayPathChecked?AIRWAY_PATH.filter((x,i)=>airwayPathAnswers[i]===x).length+'/4 correct. Answer: '+AIRWAY_PATH.join(' → '):'Choose a structure for each step.')+'</p><details><summary>Trace the gas exchange loop</summary><p><b>O₂:</b> alveoli → pulmonary capillary blood → left heart → systemic blood → tissues.</p><p><b>CO₂:</b> tissues → systemic venous blood → right heart → pulmonary capillaries → alveoli → exhaled air.</p><p>Ventilation brings air to alveoli. Perfusion brings blood to the exchange surface. Diffusion moves gases down partial-pressure gradients.</p></details></section>'+
'<section class="contentcard"><span class="tag">SLIDE 6 · CALCULATION LAB</span><h2>Minute ventilation vs alveolar ventilation</h2><p>Compare two hypothetical breathing patterns. Both move 6 L/min in total. Assume 150 mL of dead space per breath for this exercise.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Rate</th><th>Tidal volume</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>12/min</td><td>500 mL</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>30/min</td><td>200 mL</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Which delivers more fresh air to the alveoli?</p><details><summary>Show calculation and reasoning</summary><p>A: 12 × (500 − 150) = 4200 mL/min = <b>4.2 L/min</b>.</p><p>B: 30 × (200 − 150) = 1500 mL/min = <b>1.5 L/min</b>.</p><p>Equal minute ventilation can hide very different alveolar ventilation. These are simplified teaching values, not ventilation settings for a patient.</p></details><h3>Try your own values</h3><div class="toolbar"><label>Rate (breaths/min)<input id="airway-calc-rate" type="number" min="1" max="100" value="12"></label><label>Tidal volume (mL)<input id="airway-calc-tv" type="number" min="1" max="3000" value="500"></label><label>Assumed dead space (mL)<input id="airway-calc-ds" type="number" min="0" max="3000" value="150"></label></div><button onclick="airwayCalculate()">Calculate</button><p id="airway-calc-result" role="status">Minute ventilation = rate × tidal volume. Alveolar ventilation = rate × (tidal volume − dead space).</p></section>'+
'<section class="contentcard"><span class="tag">SLIDES 10–12, 18–21 · PATIENT PROBLEMS</span><h2>Apply the findings</h2><details><summary>Normal SpO₂, worsening breathing</summary><p>A patient on oxygen has SpO₂ 98%, slow shallow respirations and increasing drowsiness. Does the saturation prove adequate ventilation?</p><details><summary>Reason through it</summary><p>No. Reassess airway patency, protective ability and breathing adequacy. Supplemental oxygen may maintain saturation despite inadequate ventilation. Support breathing as indicated and reassess. Interpret capnography with the patient and waveform when available.</p></details></details><details><summary>Snoring becomes gurgling</summary><p>A patient with reduced responsiveness initially snores, then develops bubbling sounds with visible secretions. What has changed?</p><details><summary>Reason through it</summary><p>Snoring suggests soft-tissue obstruction; gurgling suggests fluid. Reassess and manage airway positioning and clearing secretions as indicated. An initially open airway may become obstructed again. Do not perform blind finger sweeps.</p></details></details><details><summary>Quieter wheezing with exhaustion</summary><p>An initially wheezing patient now has weak air movement, single-word speech and exhaustion. Is quieter breathing necessarily improvement?</p><details><summary>Reason through it</summary><p>No. Less audible wheeze may reflect reduced airflow. Reassess ABCs, breathing adequacy and mental status, support as indicated, and revisit resources and transport priority.</p></details></details></section>'+
'<section class="contentcard"><h2>Corrections and equipment context</h2><ul><li>Slide 2: pharynx order is nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx.</li><li>Slide 10: distress can include abnormal SpO₂ or color; normal oximetry does not prove normal ventilation.</li><li>Slide 13: no blind finger sweeps. Airway adjuncts require assessment, sizing and contraindication checks.</li><li>Slides 14–18: oxygen flow, CPAP, nebulizer connections and supraglottic devices depend on approved equipment, training and local protocols. The slide’s high-flow NRB example does not replace ventilatory support.</li><li>Slide 16: DuoNeb contains ipratropium plus albuterol.</li><li>Slide 19: 35–45 mmHg is a usual ETCO₂ reference, interpreted with waveform, trends and clinical context.</li></ul><div class="source">Clarification references: <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/?term=D010614" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCBI pharynx anatomy</a> · <a href="https://cpr.heart.org/en/resuscitation-science/cpr-and-ecc-guidelines/adult-basic-life-support" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AHA BLS guidance</a> · <a href="https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=3246dfab-61d7-27ba-e063-6294a90ac180" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ipratropium/albuterol labeling</a></div></section>';
}
function airwayDeckFlip(d){airwaySlideIndex=Math.max(0,Math.min(21,airwaySlideIndex+d));airwayDeckStudy()}
function airwayCheckPath(){for(let i=0;i<4;i++)airwayPathAnswers[i]=document.querySelector('#airway-path-'+i).value;airwayPathChecked=true;document.querySelector('#airway-path-status').textContent=AIRWAY_PATH.filter((x,i)=>airwayPathAnswers[i]===x).length+'/4 correct. Answer: '+AIRWAY_PATH.join(' → ')}
function airwayCalculate(){const rate=Number(document.querySelector('#airway-calc-rate').value),tv=Number(document.querySelector('#airway-calc-tv').value),ds=Number(document.querySelector('#airway-calc-ds').value),out=document.querySelector('#airway-calc-result');if(!Number.isFinite(rate)||!Number.isFinite(tv)||!Number.isFinite(ds)||rate<=0||rate>100||tv<=0||tv>3000||ds<0||ds>=tv){out.textContent='Enter rate 1–100/min, tidal volume above 0 and at most 3000 mL, and dead space at least 0 and less than tidal volume.';return}out.textContent='Minute ventilation: '+(rate*tv/1000).toFixed(2)+' L/min. Alveolar ventilation: '+(rate*(tv-ds)/1000).toFixed(2)+' L/min. Hypothetical values for learning, not patient settings.'}
